const body = `<h1>1.7a — Dice Roller: roll()</h1>

<p>
    <strong>Type:</strong> RPG Integration. This is where everything comes together.
    You've discovered the concepts, built basic applications, and now you apply it
    all to your RPG project. Instead of giving you code, this guide provides
    <strong>Acceptance Criteria</strong> — it's up to you to write code that
    satisfies the spec.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">RPG Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>The Setup</h3>

    <p>Create two files in your project:</p>
    <ol>
        <li><code>Dice.java</code> — contains all your dice logic</li>
        <li><code>DiceTester.java</code> — contains a <code>main</code> method to run your own tests</li>
    </ol>

    <p>In <code>Dice.java</code>, start with a basic class outline:</p>
    <pre><code>public class Dice {
    // Your methods will go here
}</code></pre>

    <p>
        You also need a third file, which you do not write. Download
        <a href="../data/DiceCheck.java" download>DiceCheck.java</a> and put it in the
        same folder as your <code>Dice.java</code>. It is an acceptance test: it calls
        every method you are asked to build and tells you whether each one is correct.
    </p>

    <h3>How to Run the Acceptance Test</h3>

    <p>
        Download <a href="../data/DiceCheck.java" download>DiceCheck.java</a>, put it
        beside your <code>Dice.java</code>, and from a terminal in that folder run:
    </p>
    <pre><code>javac Dice.java DiceCheck.java
java DiceCheck</code></pre>
    <p>
        The first command compiles both files together. The second runs the tests and
        prints a <code>PASS</code> or <code>FAIL</code> for every check. The last line
        counts them:
    </p>
    <pre><code>32 passed, 0 failed
Your Dice engine meets the spec.</code></pre>
    <p>
        You cannot pass all of these until you have built every method in 1.7a, 1.7b,
        and 1.7c — so expect <code>FAIL</code> lines at the start. Run it again after
        each method you finish and watch the failures shrink. The
        <a href="../docs/unit-01/dice-roller.html">full Dice Roller guide</a> walks
        through the setup in more detail.
    </p>

    <h3>Part 1: roll() — Roll a d6</h3>

    <p><strong>Requirements:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>Method signature: <code>public static int roll()</code></li>
        <li>Rolls one standard six-sided die, returning 1 through 6, inclusive</li>
        <li>This is a convenience overload — it should call <code>roll(6)</code></li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Acceptance Test:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>System.out.println("Rolling a d6: " + Dice.roll());</code></pre>
    <p>Run it several times. Do you ever see a 0? A 7?</p>

    <h3>Part 2: roll(int sides) — Roll Any Die</h3>

    <p><strong>Requirements:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>Method signature: <code>public static int roll(int sides)</code></li>
        <li>Generates a random integer between 1 and <code>sides</code> (inclusive)</li>
        <li>This is the formula you discovered in 1.3 — parameterize it</li>
        <li>This is the only method in the class that contains the random formula.
            Everything else calls it.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <p>
            Look back at your Math.random() lab. What is the "range" based on the
            <code>sides</code> parameter? What is the "min"?
        </p>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Acceptance Test:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>System.out.println("Rolling d20: " + Dice.roll(20));
System.out.println("Rolling d6:  " + Dice.roll(6));
System.out.println("Rolling d2:  " + Dice.roll(2));</code></pre>
    <p>
        Run it many times. Can <code>roll(2)</code> ever return a 3? Can
        <code>roll(20)</code> ever return 0? If so, your formula is wrong.
    </p>

    <h3>Part 3: roll(int num, int sides) — Roll Several Dice and Sum</h3>

    <p><strong>Requirements:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>Method signature: <code>public static int roll(int num, int sides)</code></li>
        <li>Rolls <code>num</code> dice, each with <code>sides</code>, and returns the total</li>
        <li><code>roll(2, 6)</code> is 2d6, so it returns 2 through 12</li>
        <li>Call your own <code>roll(sides)</code> once per die — do not write the
            random formula again</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker Challenge</h3>
        <p>
            You need to add up a new roll <code>num</code> times. How do you store a
            "running total" across repeated rolls? You need a variable that starts at 0
            and accumulates each result.
        </p>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Acceptance Test:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>System.out.println("Rolling 3d6: " + Dice.roll(3, 6));
System.out.println("Rolling 2d6: " + Dice.roll(2, 6));</code></pre>
    <p>
        Run it. Results for 3d6 should land between 3 and 18. And can 2d6 return 7?
        It should be able to — 3 + 4 is a legal pair of dice. If every 2d6 result you
        ever see is even, you are multiplying by <code>num</code> instead of adding up
        <code>num</code> separate dice.
    </p>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Write a method that parameterizes the random formula</li>
            <li>Write overloaded methods that share one name</li>
            <li>Call your own method from another method (<code>roll()</code> from
                <code>roll(sides)</code>, and <code>roll(sides)</code> from
                <code>roll(num, sides)</code>)</li>
            <li>Use a running total to accumulate multiple dice rolls</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/dice-roller.html">Student RPG Guide: Dice Roller</a> — full spec</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/math-random.html">Math.random()</a> — the formula</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
